/**
 * A planet world in a scene (specs/veml/profiles/planet.md). Loaded on
 * demand, only for a world that declares the profile.
 *
 * Floating origin: the planet hangs under one root node, turned and shifted
 * so that an anchor near the visitor is the scene's origin, with the ground's
 * up along +y, north along −z and east along −x (the sky profile's compass).
 * The first-person controller, whose gravity is −y, then walks the planet as
 * it walks any world. As the visitor moves away, the anchor moves to them.
 *
 * Terrain: the description's 3D Tiles tileset, read by tiles3d.ts and drawn
 * from glTF tiles, which enter as any glTF asset does (§4.4), in the planet's
 * frame. A visitor stands on their `ground`.
 *
 * Zoom, when the host turns it on: the mouse wheel lifts the camera from the
 * visitor's eyes to orbit and back, through the controller's external camera
 * control. Tiles are chosen by on-screen error, so the ground gives way to the
 * whole planet with no swap.
 *
 * Blocks: on a planet with the cube-sphere surface scheme, an area's chunks
 * are drawn around the visitor at every zoom, and the terrain under them is
 * cut away (areaChunks.ts).
 *
 * Held or not: in a world of several planets (planetSystem.ts), only the
 * planet that holds the visitor keeps them on its ground and draws what
 * surrounds them (its air, clouds, sun, water, blocks); one that does not
 * is drawn from its terrain alone, wherever the world puts it.
 *
 * Sites: the worlds on the planet's surface, loaded by distance from the eye
 * (planetSites.ts), under the planet's root.
 */

